---
import Page from "../../layouts/Page.astro";
import RidingHigher from "../../components/RidingHigher.astro";
import Terminal from "../../components/Terminal.astro";
import { configs, stock, tier8, mb, signed, SOUNDING_DOC } from "../../lib/trim-sounding";
import soundingMemreport from "../../examples/trim-sounding-memreport.txt?raw";
import soundingLlm from "../../examples/trim-sounding-llm.txt?raw";

const tiers = configs.slice(1);
const range = (r: [number, number]) => `${mb(r[0])}–${mb(r[1])}`;
const memoryRows: { what: string; value: (c: (typeof configs)[number]) => string; delta?: (c: (typeof configs)[number]) => number }[] = [
  { what: "Process physical memory at 60 s (memreport)", value: (c) => mb(c.mem), delta: (c) => c.mem - stock.mem },
  { what: "Peak process physical memory, 10 to 70 s (CSV profile)", value: (c) => mb(c.peak), delta: (c) => c.peak - stock.peak },
  { what: "Peak LLM tracked total (LLM platform CSV)", value: (c) => mb(c.llm), delta: (c) => c.llm - stock.llm },
  { what: "Render target pool in use (memreport)", value: (c) => mb(c.rtPool), delta: (c) => c.rtPool - stock.rtPool },
  { what: "Metal buffers (LLM platform CSV)", value: (c) => mb(c.metal), delta: (c) => c.metal - stock.metal },
];
const ranges: Record<string, (c: (typeof configs)[number]) => [number, number] | undefined> = {
  "Process physical memory at 60 s (memreport)": (c) => c.memRange,
  "Peak process physical memory, 10 to 70 s (CSV profile)": (c) => c.peakRange,
  "Peak LLM tracked total (LLM platform CSV)": (c) => c.llmRange,
  "Render target pool in use (memreport)": (c) => c.rtPoolRange,
};
const ms = (n: number) => n.toFixed(2);
const frameRows: { what: string; value: (c: (typeof configs)[number]) => string }[] = [
  { what: "Frame time, median", value: (c) => ms(c.frame) },
  { what: "Frame time, 95th percentile", value: (c) => ms(c.frameP95) },
  { what: "Frames over 50 ms (of 1800)", value: (c) => `${c.slow} [${c.slowRange[0]}–${c.slowRange[1]}]` },
  { what: "GPU time, median", value: (c) => ms(c.gpu) },
  { what: "Render thread, median", value: (c) => ms(c.render) },
  { what: "Game thread, median", value: (c) => ms(c.game) },
];
// Only report a difference where it isn't noise in the second decimal.
const frameDelta: Record<string, (c: (typeof configs)[number]) => number | undefined> = {
  "GPU time, median": (c) => c.gpu - stock.gpu,
  "Render thread, median": (c) => c.render - stock.render,
  "Game thread, median": (c) => c.game - stock.game,
};
---
<Page
  title="salvage trim, measured on a 16 GB Mac"
  compact
  description="What salvage trim's memory tiers changed in memory and frame time on Epic's Third Person template, on an Apple M4 Mac with 16 GB: 4 runs per configuration, the method, the spread, and what the numbers don't show yet."
  dek="The first sounding. Epic's Third Person template on an Apple M4 Mac with 16 GB, run stock and with each of salvage trim's three tiers, 4 times each. The 8 GB tier took 1,104 MB off the game, and the frame rate didn't move."
>
  <RidingHigher link={false} />

  <aside class="caveats" aria-labelledby="not-yet">
    <h2 id="not-yet" class="hull">What this doesn't show yet</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Real 8 and 12 GB machines.</strong> The 12 and 8 GB tiers ran on this 16 GB Mac, made to pick those
        tiers' settings. That measures what the settings save. It doesn't show how a game behaves on a machine that is
        actually short of memory, with swapping, memory compression and the OS taking memory back.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A packaged build.</strong> These runs used <code>-game</code> from the editor, which loads uncooked
        content and carries editor modules, so the absolute numbers are higher than a packaged game's. The differences
        between configurations are what this measures.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The editor preset.</strong> <code>salvage trim --editor</code> wasn't measured. It needs a session
        heavy on shader compiling, not a game run.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Settings this template doesn't exercise.</strong> The 8 GB tier's texture pool never filled (the level
        needs about 10 MB of streaming textures), there's no foliage, no streamed sound and little garbage. Those
        settings stay in the tiers because what they do is well defined, but none has a measured saving yet.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Motion, other quality levels, other machines.</strong> The player stood still, everything ran at Epic
        quality, at 1280 by 720, on one Mac with one template.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>That it fits 8 GB.</strong> Even trimmed, this run needs 453 MB more than an 8 GB machine has spare
        once a quarter is left for the OS and other apps, by <a href="#what-salvage-soundings-says"><code>salvage
        soundings</code>' reckoning</a>. A packaged build should be smaller, but we haven't measured one.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </aside>

  <div class="prose wide">
    <h2>Memory</h2>
    <p>
      Medians of 4 runs, with the lowest and highest underneath. Differences are against stock. Memory is in MB of
      1024 by 1024 bytes, as Unreal reports it.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="numbers">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">MB</th>
            {configs.map((c) => <th scope="col" class="num">{c.label}</th>)}
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {memoryRows.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{row.what}</th>
              {configs.map((c) => {
                const d = row.delta?.(c) ?? 0;
                const r = ranges[row.what]?.(c);
                return (
                  <td class="num">
                    <span class="v">{row.value(c)}</span>
                    {c.key !== "stock" && d !== 0 && <span class="d">{signed(d)}</span>}
                    {r && r[0] !== r[1] && <span class="r">{range(r)}</span>}
                  </td>
                );
              })}
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The first row decides whether a game fits a machine: it's the memory the process holds, everything included. The
      8 GB tier took it from {mb(stock.mem)} MB to {mb(tier8.mem)} MB, {signed(tier8.mem - stock.mem)} MB, and the
      peak over the run fell by almost the same, {signed(tier8.peak - stock.peak)} MB. The other rows show where the difference comes from.
    </p>

    <h2>What it costs</h2>
    <p>
      The frame rate didn't move. Every configuration held the 30 fps cap the runs used, with the same median frame
      time and nearly the same 95th percentile. Times in milliseconds over frames 300 to 2100 (10 to 70 seconds),
      medians of the per-run medians:
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="numbers">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">ms</th>
            {configs.map((c) => <th scope="col" class="num">{c.label}</th>)}
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {frameRows.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{row.what}</th>
              {configs.map((c) => {
                const d = frameDelta[row.what]?.(c);
                return (
                  <td class="num">
                    <span class="v">{row.value(c)}</span>
                    {c.key !== "stock" && d !== undefined && Math.abs(d) >= 0.1 && <span class="d">{signed(d, 2)}</span>}
                  </td>
                );
              })}
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The GPU got faster.</strong> With the 12 and 8 GB tiers, GPU time went down by about 1.6 ms, 6%. A
        smaller Lumen surface cache is less to light.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The render thread got slower.</strong> It went up by about 0.4 to 0.5 ms with the 12 and 8 GB tiers, 11
        to 15%. That's still far inside the 33 ms frame.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Slow frames.</strong> Every configuration had a handful of frames over 50 ms out of 1800. The 8 GB tier's
        median was 6 against stock's 4, with its runs ranging from 4 to 8. Four runs can't say whether that's a
        difference.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The picture.</strong> Screenshots taken at the same frame in each configuration matched stock to within
        frame-to-frame noise: a mean difference of 0.13 per 8-bit color channel, the same for the 16 GB tier (whose
        Nanite pool can't change anything in this view) as for the 12 and 8 GB tiers. The camera here sees a few metres
        of floor and one character, with no distant geometry and short shadows, so this says nothing about other views.
      </li>
      <li><strong>No warnings.</strong> None of the runs logged a shadow page pool overflow or any other pool running out.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Where the savings come from</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>16 GB tier: the Nanite streaming pool</strong>, 384 MB instead of 512 MB. The pool is allocated up
        front, so it shows as 128 MB less in Metal buffers and about 190 MB less tracked memory, in every scene.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>12 GB tier: a smaller Lumen surface cache atlas</strong> as well, 2048 instead of 4096, which took
        400 MB out of the render target pool.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>8 GB tier: fewer virtual shadow map pages</strong> as well, 512 instead of 4096. The shadow page pool
        went from 512 MB to 64 MB.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Process memory fell by 55 to 80% of the change in tracked memory, not all of it. On Apple silicon the GPU's
      memory comes out of the same RAM, so it does show up in process memory, but not one for one: Metal grows its
      heaps in 512 MB steps, and in the 8 GB run below, untagged memory grew by 448 MB while Metal textures shrank.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The 16 GB tier is bimodal.</strong> In 1 of its 4 runs, and 2 of 5 in an earlier batch, the Metal
      texture heap stayed 512 MB smaller, which put that run 500 MB lower. Stock never did this in 9 runs, and neither
      did the Nanite pool on its own in 5. It's probably where an allocation falls against the 512 MB heap step, but
      this test can't say. Go by the median, {signed(configs[1].mem - stock.mem)} MB.
    </p>

    <h2 id="what-salvage-soundings-says">What salvage soundings says</h2>
    <p>
      A stock run and an 8 GB tier run, both close to the medians, compared
      with <a href="/charts/salvage-soundings/"><code>salvage soundings</code></a>. First the memreports at 60
      seconds:
    </p>
    <Terminal
      command="salvage soundings stock.memreport tier-8gb.memreport"
      output={soundingMemreport}
      caption="An excerpt. Sizes are in GB and MB of 1024, rounded to what the tool prints."
    />
    <p>
      The 8 GB line is the honest one: trimmed or not, this run is bigger than an 8 GB machine can hold with a quarter
      left for the OS and other apps. Then the LLM platform CSVs of the same two runs:
    </p>
    <Terminal command="salvage soundings stock.csv tier-8gb.csv" output={soundingLlm} caption="An excerpt." />

    <h2>Settings that didn't make it</h2>
    <p>
      An earlier batch of 5 runs each measured every setting of the first 16 GB tier on its own, on top of stock. Some
      saved nothing, and came out:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>A lower render target pool floor</strong> (<code>r.RenderTargetPoolMin</code> of 300, 200 and 150 MB):
        7,669 MB against stock's 7,688, and no change in tracked memory. It only frees render targets that have gone
        unused, and everything in this pool is used every frame. <code>salvage soundings</code> no longer suggests it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Low-memory garbage collection</strong> (<code>gc.LowMemory.MemoryThresholdMB</code> of 2048, 1536 and
        1024 MB). It turns on when free memory falls below the threshold, and once the level was running, macOS kept
        free memory between about 1.3 and 1.9 GB. So it was on all the time in the 16 and 12 GB tiers, and saved
        nothing measurable.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Texture pool pins</strong> at 1000, 768 and 512 MB were changed before measuring. Epic's texture
        quality presets set the pool to 400, 600, 800 and 1000 MB, and a pin overrides them, so 1000 MB did nothing at
        Epic and the others added memory at lower presets. Only the 8 GB tier pins the pool now, at 400 MB.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Setup</h2>
    <table class="setup">
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Machine</th><td>Apple M4 (10-core CPU, 10-core GPU), 16 GB unified memory</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">OS</th><td>macOS 27.2 (26B123)</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Engine</th><td>Unreal Engine 5.8.4 (58902052), Epic's binary build</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Project</th><td>A fresh copy of the Third Person template (Blueprint), level <code>Lvl_ThirdPerson</code>, no changes besides <code>salvage trim</code></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Build</th><td><code>-game</code> from the editor (Development, uncooked content), not a packaged build</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Quality</th><td>Default scalability: every group at Epic</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Window</th><td>1280 by 720, windowed, capped at 30 fps</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Date</th><td>November 2026</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Method</h2>
    <p>
      Each run launched the game, left the player standing at the start of the level with the default camera, and
      quit by itself after 2100 frames, about 70 seconds:
    </p>
    <pre><code>UnrealEditor Project.uproject -game -windowed -resx=1280 -resy=720 -unattended \
  -llmcsv -ExecCmds="t.MaxFPS 30" -csvCaptureFrames=2100 -ExitAfterCsvProfiling \
  -csvExecCmds="900:memreport -full,1800:memreport -full"</code></pre>
    <p>
      That writes a memreport about 30 and 60 seconds in, LLM CSVs sampled once a second, and a CSV profile with
      per-frame timings. <a href="/charts/capturing-memory-runs/">Capture memory runs you can compare</a> explains
      the flags. A warm-up run came first, so shaders and the derived data cache were filled, and nothing else heavy ran
      on the machine.
    </p>
    <p>
      The four configurations ran interleaved, round by round, so drift over time hit all of them equally. The 16 GB
      tier needed nothing extra, since a 16 GB Mac lands in its memory bucket on its own. The 12 and 8 GB tiers used <code>-MemBucket=3</code> and <code>-MemBucket=4</code>, which put the machine in the Smaller and Smallest
      buckets. Every run's log was checked for the values its device profile pushed, so each configuration got the
      settings it was meant to.
    </p>
    <p>
      The full write-up, with every table, is <a href={SOUNDING_DOC}>in the repository</a>. To take your own,
      the <a href="/soundings/">soundings page</a> says what to record, and an issue with your numbers is welcome.
    </p>
    <p><a href="/charts/salvage-trim/">How to use salvage trim</a> has the tiers, every setting and why.</p>
  </div>
</Page>
<style>
  .caveats {
    max-width: 62rem;
    margin: 0 0 var(--s-8);
    padding: var(--s-5) 0 var(--s-5) var(--s-5);
    border-left: 6px solid var(--crane-yellow);
  }
  .caveats h2 {
    margin: 0 0 var(--s-4);
    font-size: var(--step-3);
  }
  .caveats ul {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-4);
    max-width: 48rem;
  }
  .caveats li {
    line-height: 1.55;
  }
  /* Keep flags like -game whole instead of breaking at the hyphen. */
  .caveats code,
  .prose li code {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .table-scroll {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin-block: var(--s-5);
  }
  .numbers {
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 36rem;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }
  .numbers th,
  .numbers td {
    padding: var(--s-3) var(--s-3) var(--s-3) 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    vertical-align: top;
    text-align: left;
  }
  .numbers thead th {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--text);
  }
  .numbers tbody th {
    font-weight: 600;
    min-width: 9rem;
    max-width: 16rem;
  }
  .numbers .num {
    text-align: right;
  }
  .numbers td span {
    display: block;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .numbers .v {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .numbers .d {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .numbers .r {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .setup th {
    text-align: left;
    padding-right: var(--s-4);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
</style>
